2027-01-04T13:43:25  thought   Real request, answer it properly. A short sequence she can do right now, with hold times and the one safety caveat that actually matters (radiating pain/numbness/tingling down the arm = get it looked at, not stretch it).

2027-01-04T13:43:25  message   To the owner:
Five minutes, gentle, and stop anywhere that sharpens the pain rather than just stretches it.

1. Chin tucks (1 min) — sit tall, slide your chin straight back like you're making a double chin, not tipping your head down. Hold 5 seconds, release. 10 slow reps.

2. Neck turns (1 min) — look right as far as is comfortable, hold 5 seconds, then left. 5 each way. No forcing.

3. Ear to shoulder (1 min) — drop right ear toward right shoulder, let the weight do the work, hold 20 seconds. Don't pull with your hand. 3 each side.

4. Shoulder rolls (30 sec) — big slow circles, 10 back, 10 forward. Most stiff necks are shoulders as much as neck.

5. Shoulder blade squeeze (1 min) — arms at your sides, squeeze the blades together and down, hold 5 seconds, 10 reps. This is the one that undoes desk posture.

6. Doorway chest stretch (30 sec) — forearm on the doorframe, elbow at shoulder height, step through gently. 15 seconds each side. Tight chest pulls the head forward.

Do it twice today. The one to keep up daily is the chin tucks and blade squeezes.

One caveat: if the soreness comes with tingling or numbness down an arm, or you get dizzy or it started after a knock, don't stretch it — get it looked at.

