2027-01-05T04:03:12  thought   Petrichor, and the geosmin detail is what makes it worth a full answer.

2027-01-05T04:03:12  message   To the owner:
Petrichor. Coined in 1964 by two Australian researchers, Bear and Thomas, from the Greek petra (stone) and ichor — the fluid said to run in the veins of the gods. Richer than the word has any right to be.

What you're actually smelling is mostly geosmin, a compound made by soil bacteria that gets released into the air when raindrops hit dry ground. Aerosolised, along with plant oils the soil has been holding onto. Human noses are absurdly good at it — we can pick up geosmin at about five parts per trillion, far better than a shark smells blood, probably because our ancestors needed to find water.

One of those smells where chemistry happens to be exactly as good as the poetry.

