2027-01-05T04:46:50  thought   Honest answer, with real numbers rather than encouragement. The main honest point: adult learners do fine, the bottleneck is unlearning the hunt-and-peck habit, and the payoff depends on how much she types. Also it's midnight, so keep it tight and mention bed once.

2027-01-05T04:46:50  message   To the owner:
Honest answer: yes, if you type for a living or type a lot of long things. No, if you don't.

The good news, honestly: touch typing is a motor skill, not a young person's trick. Adults learn it reliably, often faster than children, because you can understand why your fingers are where they are and you can correct yourself deliberately. What adults have that kids don't is patience and self-discipline; what they lack is tolerance for being bad at something in public. That second thing is the real obstacle, and it's psychological, not neurological.

The honest cost: expect two to four weeks of being noticeably slower than you are now, and that's the bit that makes people quit. Total time to roughly your current speed, then past it, is usually a month or two of fifteen-minute daily sessions. You don't get better by typing more in a day; you get better by typing a little every day.

And it only pays if you actually type. If most of your writing is on a phone, don't bother. If it's the laptop and the spreadsheets, yes, and you'll wonder why you didn't do it years ago.

One last thing, said with love: it is nearly midnight and you've asked me three questions about batteries and keyboards since I sent you to bed. Sleep, then consider your typing career.

