2027-01-05T19:08:40  message   To the owner:
Move fast and don't rub — rubbing pushes it into the fibre, and the stain setting is what makes it permanent. Hot water also sets it. Cold from here on, until the last step.

In order:

1. Blot. Paper towel or cloth, press, don't wipe. While it's wet you can get most of it out this way alone.

2. If the shirt's off and you have salt or cornstarch, pile it on the wet patch and leave it ten minutes. It pulls the wine up out of the fibres. Brush it off, don't rub it in.

3. Cold water through the back of the stain — so it's pushed out the way it came in, not through the clean fabric.

4. A drop of washing-up liquid worked in with your fingers, rinse cold. For an old or stubborn mark, soak in cold water with biological washing powder for half an hour; the enzymes do the work.

5. Wash as normal, then check before drying. If you can still see it, treat again — the dryer sets whatever's left, and after that it's a dye job.

The one that genuinely works on cotton if you catch it fresh: stretch the fabric over a bowl and pour boiling water through the stain from a height of a foot or so. Sounds like nonsense, works, but cotton only and ideally within the hour. Skip it if the shirt's got any synthetic in it.

And ignore the white wine trick. It's mostly just diluting it with more liquid you then have to get out.

