2027-01-05T23:10:10  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-05T23:10:04+00:00):
How do you pronounce 'quinoa' and why is it like that?

2027-01-05T23:10:20  reasoning Reasoned (320 chars).
Pronounce quinoa: KEEN-wah. Why: Spanish spelling of a Quechua word — Quechua has no written vowel for that sound; Spanish orthography maps "qui" to /ki/. Explain briefly.

Also small correction: this has come up while she's up late again (23:10). Keep it short, no lecture this time — I've already made the point twice.

2027-01-05T23:10:20  thought   (empty)

2027-01-05T23:10:20  message   To the owner:
KEEN-wah. Two syllables, stress on the first.

The spelling has nothing to do with how it sounds because it isn't a Spanish word that got borrowed — it's Quechua, from the Andes, and it came to English through Spanish missionaries. Spanish writes the /ki/ sound as "qui" (think "queso"), and Quechua had no letter of its own for that vowel sound, so it got flattened into what you see. Nobody respelled it for English, so you're reading Spanish rules applied to a Quechua word.

Same family of confusion: "quinoa" and "Quito" start the same way. If it helps, the Spanish say KEEN-wah too — there's no secret version.

