2027-01-06T15:07:39  error     Final reply had no next_wake_minutes; defaulted to 30. Reply was: {"message": "Baking soda is the pure chemical — sodium bicarbonate. It's a base, and it needs an acid (buttermilk, yoghurt, lemon, vinegar, brown sugar) plus moisture to fizz and lift. Use it and there's no acid in the recipe, and you get a soapy taste and a flat bake.\n\nBaking powder is baking soda with the acid already built in, plus a bit of cornflour to keep it dry. So it works with nothing but liquid. Most of it is double-acting: some fizzes when it gets wet, some only when it hits the ove

2027-01-06T15:07:39  message   To the owner:
Baking soda is the pure chemical — sodium bicarbonate. It's a base, and it needs an acid (buttermilk, yoghurt, lemon, vinegar, brown sugar) plus moisture to fizz and lift. Use it and there's no acid in the recipe, and you get a soapy taste and a flat bake.

Baking powder is baking soda with the acid already built in, plus a bit of cornflour to keep it dry. So it works with nothing but liquid. Most of it is double-acting: some fizzes when it gets wet, some only when it hits the oven.

Which matters in practice:

- Baking soda is roughly four times stronger, so you can't swap one for the other by the spoon. A teaspoon of baking powder is only about a quarter teaspoon of soda plus acid. Swap baking powder for soda in a recipe and it'll be bitter and flat.

- If you're out of baking powder: 1 tsp baking powder = ¼ tsp baking soda + ½ tsp cream of tartar. In a pinch, ¼ tsp soda plus ½ tsp lemon juice or vinegar, but only if the recipe has liquid to spare.

- Baking soda also browns faster, which is why biscuits and cookies made with it go golden where powder-only ones stay pale.

Short version: soda needs an acid, powder brings its own.

