2027-01-06T15:23:11  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-06T15:23:05+00:00):
Can you explain the difference between a mortgage rate and an APR, briefly?

2027-01-06T15:23:21  reasoning Reasoned (30 chars).
Just answer briefly, as asked.

2027-01-06T15:23:21  thought   (empty)

2027-01-06T15:23:21  message   To the owner:
The rate is the interest on the money you're borrowing. The APR is that rate plus the fees, shown as one yearly number so you can compare offers.

So: 4.5% rate with £2,000 of fees could be 4.9% APR. Same loan, different number.

Two practical points. APR is the one to compare lenders by, because it's the only figure that includes the cost of arranging the thing. But a low APR on a long fixed term isn't automatically better than a higher one on a short term — total cost depends on term too, so look at both.

One caveat: on a mortgage, APR also assumes you keep it for the full term, which most people don't. The rate matters more if you plan to move or remortgage before it ends.

