2027-01-06T15:25:44  message   To the owner:
They're cheap enough new that used only makes sense for a good brand — a Dualit, a Stelton, a nice glass one — rather than saving four pounds on a supermarket kettle.

What to check before you pay:

- The element and the base of the inside. Limescale is normal and comes off with citric acid or vinegar; hard crusted white scale round the element or a chalky rim at the waterline means it's been boiled in hard water for years, and that's the thing that shortens a kettle's life. Look for a metal element plate that isn't pitted.
- Boil it. Fill to the minimum, switch on, time it, and make sure it clicks off by itself. A kettle that doesn't cut out is a fire risk, and that's the one fault worth walking away from even at a good price. Do it twice — some only stick on a second boil.
- The lid and the switch. Lid that doesn't sit flush or a switch that feels mushy and needs a press to catch are the two most common failures on old kettles, and both are annoying in daily use.
- Smell and taste. Fill, boil, pour a cup and smell it. A kettle that's had years of the same water can taste of plastic or of nothing much; a quick rinse with a citric acid solution sorts most of it, but if it's had something else in it, you'll know.
- The inside material. Stainless steel ages well; plastic linings yellow and can hold a taste; glass is lovely but you can see every flaw, which is either an advantage or a reason to walk away.
- Cord and plug. Frayed flex, a plug with a cracked casing, or a cut cord someone has shortened — no.

Then the boring bits: does a new one cost £15, and is the saving worth no warranty and no returns? And on the hygiene side, whatever you buy, run a 50/50 water-and-white-vinegar boil or a citric acid descale before the first cup.

What's the kettle and what are they asking? If it's a brand name I can tell you whether that model has a known fault worth checking.

