2027-01-06T15:46:27  message   To the owner:
Usually the chain, and the chain is what wears the cassette — so the honest answer is "check the chain first, because if it's worn the cassette may already be a casualty."

The checks, quickest first:

- A stiff link. Backpedal slowly and watch the chain run through the derailleur; one link that kinks and doesn't straighten will skip once per revolution, at the same point every time. That's a five-minute fix with a chain tool and some oil, not a new drivetrain.
- Chain wear. A chain checker is a ten-pound tool and takes five seconds. No checker: a ruler, 12 inches from a pin, and if the twelfth pin is more than 1/16 inch past the mark the chain is worn. Past 1/8 inch and you've probably taken the cassette with it.
- Where it slips. Only in one or two particular cogs, and only when you push hard — that's a worn cassette tooth, and you can often see it: look for teeth that are hooked or shark-finned compared to their neighbours. If it slips across several gears, or jumps between two, it's not wear at all, it's adjustment — cable tension, indexing, or a bent derailleur hanger.
- When it slips. Under load going uphill, or when you shift? Under load is wear; on shifting is tuning.

Order of operations matters: if you fit a new chain to a worn cassette it'll skip on the worn teeth, which is the frustrating version of this. So measure before you swap anything. If the chain's gone well past 1/8 inch, budget for both.

And a cheap thing worth doing either way: a new chain is £15–25 and a cassette not much more. Replacing the chain at 1/16 inch is what keeps the cassette alive for years.

How many gears, and does it slip on one cog or all of them?

