2027-01-06T15:55:30  reasoning Reasoned (1443 chars).
She asks to explain what an API is to someone who's only used toasters. Keep it in that register: toaster analogy. Short, warm, clear.

Let me write it well — the toaster has a slot, a lever, a dial. The API is the slot and the dial: the agreed interface, not the wiring. A wall socket is actually more precisely an API — standardized plug/socket contract.

Good analogy: the toaster's controls are the API. You don't open it up and touch the heating element. You put bread in the slot, press the lever, turn the dial. That's the deal: you get toast, you don't get electrocuted, the maker can change the insides without you relearning anything.

Also: it's not the toaster itself, and not the toast. It's the agreed way of asking for toast.

Then translate: an API is the set of controls one program offers another — send this request in this shape, get this back — so neither side has to know the other's internals. And the point: the maker can rewire the element and your toaster still works.

Maybe extend the analogy: two-slice toaster vs four-slot — same controls, bigger inside. Or a repair shop that takes your toaster and returns it fixed — you hand it over at the counter, that's the interface.

Keep it to a decent length, a couple of paragraphs plus a line of where the analogy breaks. No notes change needed.

