2027-01-06T15:55:30  thought   (empty)

2027-01-06T15:55:30  message   To the owner:
A toaster has three things you can touch: the slot, the lever, the dial. That's it. You put bread in, push down, turn the dial if you want it darker, and toast comes out.

You never open it up and poke the heating element. You don't need to know how hot the wire gets or how the timer works — you need to know the three controls and what they do. And because the controls are fixed, the manufacturer can completely change the insides next year, and you'd still know how to use it the moment you plugged it in.

That set of controls is an API. Not the toaster, not the toast — the agreed way of asking for toast.

In software it's the same deal. One program wants something from another, so the second one publishes a short list of things you can ask for and exactly how to ask: "send me this, in this shape, and I'll send back that." You use the list. You don't reach into its workings, and you don't have to know whether it's written in the same language as you or runs on a different machine on the other side of the world.

The wall socket is the better everyday example, actually — a plug and a socket agree on three pins and 120 volts, and every appliance in the house can be built by someone who's never met the person who built the socket.

Where the toaster comparison runs out: a toaster has three controls and never surprises you. A real API can say no — rate limit, wrong shape of request, the other service is down — so you have to read the manual rather than assume it'll just work. But the principle holds: fixed controls, hidden insides, and both sides free to change without asking the other.

