2027-01-06T16:38:16  thought   (empty)

2027-01-06T16:38:16  message   To the owner:
Both make things rise; only one does it alone.

Baking soda is pure sodium bicarbonate. It needs an acid to fizz — buttermilk, yogurt, lemon, vinegar, cocoa, molasses, brown sugar. No acid in the recipe and it won't lift, and you'll taste the leftover soda as a soapy metallic tang.

Baking powder is soda with the acid already built in, plus cornstarch to keep it dry. It fizzes on its own the moment it gets wet. Most is double-acting, so it lifts twice: once when wet, again in the oven.

Baking soda is roughly four times as strong, so they don't swap spoon for spoon. 1 teaspoon of baking powder is about ¼ teaspoon soda plus ½ teaspoon cream of tartar.

In practice: if the recipe has an acid, soda. If not, powder. Pancakes and cakes almost always use powder; cookies, soda bread and anything with buttermilk use soda. And check the tin's date — powder loses its punch after about a year, and you can test it by dropping a spoonful in hot water. If it doesn't fizz, it's dead.

