2027-01-06T20:36:20  message   To the owner:
Three, and the second is the one I'd lead with.

Octopuses punch fish. Not metaphorically. They hunt alongside fish — groupers, snappers — and when a fish is being unhelpful, hogging a crevice or not pulling its weight, the octopus will throw an arm out and sock it. Filmed repeatedly. Sometimes it looks like pure spite. Nobody's sure it isn't.

They have no bones. The only hard part is the beak, which is about the size of your thumbnail, so an octopus can pour itself through any hole bigger than that. A 40kg giant Pacific octopus can fit through a two-inch gap. It does this slowly and deliberately, and it's genuinely unsettling to watch.

And their skin is a display, driven directly by nerves — no hormones involved, unlike the rest of the animal kingdom. Each colour cell has a muscle attached, so they can think "dark patch here" and have it happen in under a second. They can also raise bumps and spikes to match texture, which most colour-changers can't. Effectively they have a screen they control pixel by pixel.

There's a fourth thing about sleep and colour that I'll save unless you want it, because it's the strangest and I'd rather you asked.

