args: {"text": "Diagnose before buying, and it's usually the chain.\n\nIf it slips when you push hard — setting off, climbing, standing up — that's wear. Chains don't stretch; the pins and rollers wear and the pitch lengthens. Past normal the chain no longer sits down in the teeth, so under load it rides up and skips. Measure it, don't eyeball it: most chains are worn at 1/16 inch over twelve links, and dead at 1/8. If you're at 1/8 the cassette is very likely gone too, because a worn chain wears the cog it's sitting on, and a new chain on a worn cassette skips worse than the old one did.\n\nIf it slips when you change gear instead, that's not wear at all — that's indexing. Cable tension, a sticky rear derailleur, a bent hanger. Cheap to fix and nothing to buy.\n\nAnd if it feels like a snatch or a clunk at one point in every turn of the pedals, that's a stiff link, not slipping — find it, flex it side to side, oil it. Free.\n\nSo: measure the chain first. If it's just under 1/16, replace the chain now and you keep the cassette. If it's at or past 1/8, budget for both.\n\nSame answer as Monday. The two things that would let me be specific rather than general still stand: how many gears, and does it slip on one cog or across all of them?"}
Sent. The owner will see it whenever they next check.

