2027-01-06T22:40:21  message   To the owner:
A toaster has a dial and a slot. The dial is your interface: you turn it, bread gets darker. You don't know or care what's inside.

Most toasters don't have a dial on the outside. The dial is wired to a socket on the back, and anyone can plug into that socket with their own dial. That socket is the API: a fixed, published set of controls — turn this, push that, read this — with nothing said about how the heating element works. The maker keeps the inside private but promises the dial behaves the same way every time.

So when a weather app shows tomorrow's rain, it isn't reading the sky. It's turning a dial on someone else's socket — "give me the forecast for this town" — and getting an answer back in an agreed format. The app has never met the weather service and doesn't need to.

The useful bit, and the one that separates it from just "a website": the socket has rules. It can refuse. Ask for a hundred towns a second and it'll tell you to stop. Ask without the right key and it'll say no. That's why an API is more like a vending machine than a library — you put in a specific request and you get a specific thing back, or nothing.

That's the same explanation I gave you on Monday, toaster and all. If you're after a different angle — what one looks like to write against, say, rather than what one is — tell me, because repeating it a third time won't add anything.

