2027-01-07T14:22:45  thought   (empty)

2027-01-07T14:22:45  message   To the owner:
The gist: a retirement account where you pay the tax now and nothing later.

You put in money that's already been taxed, it grows untaxed, and when you take it out in retirement the whole lot — contributions and growth — is tax-free. Unlike a traditional IRA, where you get a deduction going in and pay tax coming out.

That trade suits you if you expect to be in a higher tax bracket later, or if you just want to know the number you're pulling out is the number you get. The catch is you get no deduction now, there's a yearly cap on what you can put in, and touching the earnings before you're 59½ costs you tax plus a penalty. You can always take out what you put in.

Same answer as Monday, gist and all. That's the second time you've asked it this week — happy to keep giving it, but if it's because you're actually deciding something about one, tell me the choice and I'll answer that instead.

