2027-01-07T14:37:27  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-07T14:37:17+00:00):
What's a good way to stop my laptop fan from spinning up all the time?

2027-01-07T14:37:37  reasoning Reasoned (248 chars).
She asks about laptop fan. Answer practically. Also note the repeat pattern — this was asked Monday. Give proper answer: dust, vents, power settings, background processes, thermal paste for older machines, undevolting. Keep it useful and short-ish.

2027-01-07T14:37:37  thought   (empty)

2027-01-07T14:37:37  message   To the owner:
Most likely dust, and it's the only one you can fix in ten minutes.

Start there. Shut it down, and blow compressed air through the vents in short bursts — a few seconds at a time, because long blasts spin the fan past what it likes. Better still, hold the fan still with a cocktail stick while you do it, since it's the spinning that does the damage. On a laptop more than about eighteen months old, that alone usually halves it. If you're comfortable opening the back, that's where the real felt mat of dust is, and it's four screws.

Then the software things, in order of payoff. Look at what's running: a browser with forty tabs, a backup app, or a sync client churning on something will keep one core pinned and the fan on. Same for a laptop sitting on a duvet or a cushion — the vents are underneath and they need air; hard flat surface or a stand. And Windows or macOS power settings quietly do a lot in the other direction: power-saving mode on the processor is the single biggest scriptable change to fan noise, and the machine usually feels no slower for it.

What I wouldn't do: put it on a cooling pad with extra fans (moves the noise, doesn't fix it), or run a "cleaning" app.

Last thing, and it's the one worth acting on: if it's old enough that the air and the settings don't help, the thermal paste between chip and heatsink has dried out. That's a repair, not a chore — but it's cheap and it's the actual cause.

Same advice as Monday. That's the third repeat this morning after the Roth IRA and touch typing — the record's holding, so nothing's drifting.

