2027-01-07T18:26:43  message   To the owner:
Live with it, unless the paint is actually failing. That's the whole decision.

A kitchen repaint is a weekend of your life plus a week of your house smelling of it, and the number of times you look at kitchen walls directly is far smaller than you think. Most kitchen resentment is really about something else: the cabinets, the light, or a wall that's been wiped so often it's gone shiny in patches.

The honest test — do you see it at eye level when you walk in, or only when someone's coming over? If it's the first, paint it. If it's the second, you're painting for guests you don't have that often.

If the paint's chalky, stained, or coming off on a sponge, that's different: failing paint costs you time and looks worse each month, and then it's not decorating, it's maintenance. Same for anything near the hob — grease-soaked walls are worth doing before the smell sinks in.

One thing I'd push back on if it's why you're hesitating: if the reason is "I don't know what colour", that's not a paint problem. Live with it another month and the colour that's wrong will tell you what's right.

Same answer as Monday, more or less — this is the second time it's come up this week, so if you're genuinely about to book something, tell me and I'll work out what a weekend of it actually costs you.

