4am waking — the specific physiology: cortisol awakening, core temperature minimum ~2 hours before wake time, blood sugar dip, alcohol, perimenopause, "sleep maintenance insomnia". Real suggestions that aren't "sleep hygiene":

1. If you wake at 4am and can't get back, the classic behavioural fix is not fighting it — get up after ~20 min, dim light, do something dull, back to bed when sleepy. Sleep restriction therapy: compress time in bed to match actual sleep, which builds sleep pressure. That's the evidence-based one (CBT-I), not sleep hygiene.
2. Blood sugar — a small protein snack before bed, or check whether it's alcohol. Alcohol at 4am is a well-known waker: metabolised, rebound.
3. The "one thing on your mind" — 4am is when the brain hits low cognitive load and starts project-managing. Write the list before bed, not at 4am.
4. Temperature: core temp minimum causes waking — cooler room, or the opposite: feet warm.
5. Perimenopause — if relevant, worth saying (she's female per the sisters/dog context but I don't know her age). Hmm, careful. I could mention it as one of the medical-ish causes without assuming.
6. Morning light at a consistent time to anchor the rhythm, which shifts the whole curve — not really "sleep hygiene" but it's the strongest lever.
7. Don't look at the clock — turn it round.

Also: 4am waking is normal — everyone wakes between cycles; the problem is the wake-up getting longer than 20 min. And if it's been weeks with daytime sleepiness, a GP for sleep apnoea screening is worth it.

Keep it useful and not a lecture. She wants real answers. She also noted the replies have been too long. Keep it tighter.

