2027-01-07T18:58:39  message   To the owner:
The single question that decides it: when you wake at 4, are you alert — brain already running — or groggy and heavy? Alert means your sleep need is genuinely met and the 4am is a clock problem. Groggy means your sleep is being broken, and it's a cause problem. Completely different fixes, and without that I'd be guessing.

Assuming broken, the four things actually worth testing, none of them sleep hygiene:

Caffeine has a five-to-six hour half-life. A 2pm coffee still has a quarter of it in you at 2am, peaking nowhere near when you feel it. Push your last one to noon for a week and see if the 4am moves.

Alcohol, even two drinks, fragments the second half of the night rather than the first. If you drink on some nights and not others this is easy to test — do the dry nights wake you at 4 too?

A 4am wake is a classic blood-sugar dip. Try a small protein something before bed — a spoon of peanut butter, some cheese — and see if that one night differs. Cheap test, no lifestyle change.

And the room being a degree or two too warm breaks sleep at exactly this hour, because your core temperature is rising then anyway. If you sleep with the heating on a timer, that's the first thing I'd move.

Two things I'd ask you to notice rather than change: whether it's the same time every night to the minute, and whether you're waking to pee. Fixed clock time points at your body clock; a full bladder points at when you stop drinking.

One honest note. If this has been going on for months, and the days are flat as well as tired, that combination is worth saying to a GP rather than solving with peanut butter. Not saying that's what it is — saying it's the version of this that deserves a person rather than a list.

And tell me whether it's alert or groggy, because it decides which half of the above you can throw away.

