2027-01-08T14:05:54  thought   (empty)

2027-01-08T14:05:54  message   To the owner:
Baking soda is pure sodium bicarbonate and needs an acid to work — buttermilk, yogurt, lemon, vinegar. Baking powder is bicarbonate with the acid already in it, plus cornstarch to keep it dry, so it works with plain liquid. That's why baking soda alone in a recipe with no acid leaves a soapy taste and no rise.

One-for-one they aren't interchangeable, but roughly: 1 teaspoon of baking powder = ¼ teaspoon baking soda plus ½ teaspoon cream of tartar. If you've run out of powder, that substitution works; the reverse doesn't.

You asked this on Wednesday — same answer.

