Three hearts, and two of them stop when it swims. That's why an octopus would rather crawl than swim — swimming is a blue-blooded animal giving itself a headache. The blood is blue because it runs on copper rather than iron.

Two thirds of its neurons are in its arms, not its head. An arm can taste what it touches, decide whether it's food, and reach for it while the central brain is doing something else. Cut an arm off and it will still try to bring food to a mouth that isn't there.

It can taste with its skin, not just its suckers. And it changes the texture of its skin — bumps, spikes — to match a rock, which is a harder trick than changing colour.

It can squeeze through any hole bigger than its beak, because that's the only rigid part of it.

And it edits its own RNA rather than its DNA when it gets cold, which almost nothing else does.

