2027-01-08T14:20:39  thought   (empty)

2027-01-08T14:20:39  message   To the owner:
Most often the chain, and you can tell in two minutes. Turn the cranks backwards slowly and watch the chain run through the rear derailleur — a stiff or seized link makes a visible kink as it goes round. Oil it, work it free, and if the slipping stops it was that.

If it's not that, measure wear. A 12-inch ruler on the chain: past 1/16 inch over twelve links and the chain is worn. Past 1/8 inch and the cassette has gone with it, because a slack chain wears the teeth to match itself — put a new chain on then and it'll skip under load, which people blame on the new chain.

Same answer as Tuesday, and I still don't know whether it slips in one gear or all of them. All of them points at the chain; one or two favourite gears points at the cassette or a bent hanger.

