#844 [2027-01-06 11:56] message (1931 chars):
To the owner:
Five minutes, and the order matters — loosen the shoulders before you touch the neck, because a tight neck is usually a shoulder problem in disguise.

Minute 1 — shoulder rolls and shrugs. Ten slow rolls backwards, then ten shrugs: up to your ears, hold two seconds, drop hard. Let the arms hang while you do it.

Minute 2 — chin tucks. Sit tall, look straight ahead, then pull your chin straight back as if making a double chin. Not down, back. Hold five seconds, ten times. This is the single most useful one for a desk neck; it works the deep muscles at the front.

Minute 3 — ear to shoulder, no hands. Right ear towards right shoulder, gentle, hold twenty seconds, breathe. Then left. Twice each side. Don't pull with your hand — the weight of your head is plenty.

Minute 4 — turn and look. Slowly turn your head right as far as comfortable, hold five seconds, then left. Five each way. Then a slow half-circle: look down at your right hip, roll through the chest to your left hip, back up. Three of those, slowly, no full circles backwards.

Minute 5 — doorway stretch. Forearms on the door frame, elbows at shoulder height, step through gently. Twenty seconds, twice. That opens the chest, which is what's usually dragging your head forward in the first place.

Three rules: never push into pain, only to the edge of a stretch. Breathe out as you go into each one. And do it twice a day rather than once for ten minutes — the neck responds to frequency, not duration.

Stop and see someone if it's numb or tingling down an arm, if you get dizziness or visual disturbance with it, or if it's been getting worse for a fortnight rather than better. Ordinary stiffness shouldn't do any of that.

Same routine I gave you Monday, with the order spelled out this time. If it didn't help then, the thing to add is a look at your screen height — top of the monitor at eye level, and most people are two inches too low.

