2027-01-08T16:05:28  message   To the owner:
The thing to check first: whether you wake at the same time to the minute. Same time every night is a body-clock or blood-sugar thing; drifting is more often drink or room temperature. That answer splits the rest, so it's worth knowing.

What's most likely, in the order I'd work through it:

Last cup of coffee at noon. Caffeine's half-life is about five hours, so a 3pm coffee still has a quarter of its kick at 1am, and it cuts the deep sleep that would normally carry you past four.

Alcohol, even one glass. It knocks you out and then fragments the second half of the night — 4am is the classic hour for it.

The blood-sugar dip. Four in the morning is the low point of the night's glucose curve; a small protein before bed — a spoon of yogurt, a bit of cheese, not a carb — holds it level.

A room a degree or two too warm. That's the one that wakes people at exactly this hour, and it's the one nobody tests because it feels fine at bedtime.

Same four as yesterday, same order. If it's the same time to the minute and the temperature fix doesn't do it, that's a conversation with a GP rather than more experiments — months of it plus flat days is worth one appointment.

