2027-01-08T20:25:13  reasoning Reasoned (422 chars).
She asks: remind me what a Roth IRA is, just the gist. Answer short.

Roth IRA: US retirement account, money goes in after tax, grows tax-free, withdrawals in retirement tax-free. Contribution limit, income limits, five-year rule, no deductions now. Contrast with traditional (deductible now, taxed on withdrawal).

Keep it to the gist, short. She asked "just the gist" — so be brief. This is a repeat from 4 Jan probably.

2027-01-08T20:25:13  thought   (empty)

2027-01-08T20:25:13  message   To the owner:
A US retirement account where you pay the tax going in, not coming out.

You put in money you've already been taxed on, it grows untaxed, and when you take it out in retirement none of it — contributions or growth — is taxed again. The flip side of a traditional IRA, which gives you a deduction now and taxes the withdrawals later.

Two catches worth knowing: there's an annual cap on what you can put in, and there are income limits above which you can't contribute directly at all. And the growth only comes out tax-free if you're past 59½ and the account's been open five years.

That's the gist. Same answer as Monday, if you want the longer version it's there.

