2027-01-08T21:20:36  message   To the owner:
Same answer as Thursday, in the same order.

Small and established beats big and cheap. A modest pothos, spider plant, snake plant or zz plant in decent soil will outlive a larger half-dead one from a supermarket shelf — and the pot matters more than the plant for how it reads as a gift.

What that means in practice: £8-15 on the plant, and if you can spend anything extra, spend it on the pot rather than the plant. Terracotta or a plain ceramic reads as chosen; shiny foil doesn't.

If they already have plants, the better gift is a small bag of decent potting mix or a nice pair of snips — anyone who likes plants has a use for both and no one ever thinks to buy them. If you have no idea what they own, a pothos is the safe one: it survives a desk with no window.

Skip cut flowers, skip anything in a novelty pot, skip a cactus if they have a cat.

