2027-01-10T04:14:36  message   To the owner:
A toaster has a dial and a slot. The dial is your interface: you turn it, bread gets darker, and you don't know or care what's inside.

An API is the dial. A website is for a human — menus, buttons, pictures of toast. An API is the same service with the pictures and menus stripped off, leaving a short list of things you're allowed to ask for: "toast this, setting 3." You send the request, it sends back the result, and nothing else is on offer.

The point of the dial existing separately is that anything can turn it. A person, an app, a script at 3am. They all have to use the same dial and the same settings — which is why an API is strict about exactly how you phrase the request, and why it's the same for everybody.

The one thing to check first: an API is not a back door. It only exposes what the service decided to expose. If there's no dial marked "defrost", you can't defrost, however hard you turn it.

Same answer as Wednesday, same order.

