2027-01-10T04:15:39  message   To the owner:
Same as Wednesday, same order.

Check the element first, because it's the thing you can't fix. A bare metal coil in the bottom will fur up and eventually die; a flat concealed base is easier to live with. Look at the bottom of the inside — white crust is limescale, and a thick layer means it's been boiling hard water for years. A cup of white vinegar and a boil will tell you if it comes off.

Then smell it. Hot plastic or a burnt tang that doesn't go away after two boils is the kettle, not the water.

Then the cheap bits: lid seals that have gone hard, a switch that sticks, a cord cut short, and whether it actually clicks off when it should. Fill it, boil it, pour it out — all three, in front of the seller.

And ask why they're selling. A kettle that's a spare or a house move is one thing; "it leaks a bit" is your answer.

The one thing to check first: the inside bottom. Everything else is replaceable, that isn't.

