2027-01-10T04:17:13  message   To the owner:
Baby behaviour that never got switched off.

Kittens push their front paws against their mother's belly to make the milk come faster. The motion gets wired in early, and adult cats keep it as a leftover comfort behaviour — the same category as a person tapping a foot.

It comes with two extras. There are scent glands between the pads, so kneading is also claiming the blanket as theirs. And it's often paired with purring and suckling the fabric, which is the kitten part showing through most plainly.

Why the blanket and not the floor: soft, warm, gives slightly under the paws — as close to a belly as furniture gets.

Nothing to do about it except put a throw over anything you'd rather not have punctured. Same answer as Monday.

