2027-01-10T05:04:53  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T05:04:53  message   To the owner:
The rate is what you pay on the balance. The APR is that plus the fees, spread over the whole term and expressed as one yearly number.

So a 5% rate with £2,000 of arrangement fees on a £200,000 loan might show as 5.2% APR. Same monthly payment; the APR just tells you what the deal costs once the fees are in.

The practical use: compare APRs, not rates, when two deals have different fees. A lower rate with big fees can be the worse deal, and the APR is the only number that puts them on the same footing.

Where it misleads: the APR assumes you keep the mortgage to the end of the term. If you'll remortgage or move in three years, the fees hit you once and the rate hits you every month — so run the total cost over your actual horizon and ignore the APR.

The one thing to check first: whether the fee is added to the loan or paid upfront. Added, you pay interest on it for thirty years.

This is the one that went missing last night when my reply failed twice — sorry about that.

