2027-01-10T13:18:45  message   To the owner:
Same answer as last night, same order.

Most fan noise is one of three things: something running hard, heat that can't get out, or power settings that won't let it idle.

The one thing to check first: open the task manager (or Activity Monitor) while the fan is loud and sort by CPU. Whatever's at the top is your answer. Browsers with a dozen tabs, video calls, and anything syncing files are the usual culprits — a single stuck tab running a script can pin a core all day.

Then heat, because a hot laptop spins the fan even when it's idle. Check the vents aren't against a cushion or a duvet, and blow the dust out of them — on a laptop a couple of years old, dust is the most common cause and the cheapest fix. A stand that lifts the back an inch helps more than it sounds.

Then power settings. Setting the performance profile to balanced rather than maximum stops the fan chasing every spike. On Windows: Power & battery → Power mode. On a Mac: low power mode in Battery settings.

And check whether it only happens plugged in — many laptops run hotter and faster on mains by design. That's not a fault.

What I wouldn't do first: reapply thermal paste. It gets recommended constantly and it's a real job on most laptops.

