2027-01-10T21:52:57  message   To the owner:
Baking soda is the pure chemical — sodium bicarbonate. It's a base, so it needs an acid (buttermilk, yoghurt, lemon juice, vinegar, brown sugar) plus moisture to fizz and lift. Use it where there's no acid and you get a soapy taste and a flat bake.

Baking powder is baking soda with the acid already built in, plus a bit of cornflour to keep it dry. So it works with nothing but liquid. Most of it is double-acting: some fizzes when it gets wet, the rest only when it hits the oven.

Three practical numbers:

- Soda is roughly four times as strong as powder. A teaspoon of soda does what four teaspoons of powder would.
- To swap powder for soda: 1 tsp powder = ¼ tsp soda plus ½ tsp something acidic (cream of tartar, a squeeze of lemon).
- To swap soda for powder: don't, unless the recipe also has an acid — then use three to four times as much powder and accept a slightly different crumb.

The one thing to check first: powder lasts months, soda lasts years, but both die. A teaspoon of either into hot water should fizz. If it doesn't, it's not going to lift anything.

