[Wed 06 Jan 2027 08:46] You: Three, picked for a long drive specifically — nothing that needs watching, nothing where you have to start at episode one.

Funny: No Such Thing As A Fish. Four people who research facts for a living, each brings one a week, and they talk rubbish around it. It's the QI research team, it's been running for years, and there are hundreds of episodes so a drive barely dents it. You can start literally anywhere. This is the one to put on if the other two feel like homework.

Story, funny and quietly devastating: Heavyweight. Jonathan Goldstein helps people go back and resolve something they've been carrying around for years — a friendship that ended badly, a brother who vanished, a borrowed car. Half an hour each, self-contained, and it's the best-made thing on this list.

Long and absorbing: Hardcore History. Dan Carlin, one subject for three or four hours at a stretch — the Mongols, the First World War, the fall of the Roman Republic. It's genuinely what the format was invented for. Not funny, but it makes six hours feel like two, and you'll come out knowing about the Bronze Age collapse.

