2027-01-11T01:18:07  reasoning Reasoned (694 chars).
The touch typing question — asked before (4 Jan, and maybe repeated). Final reply should note repeat. Keep short.

I recall from MEMORY.md: 2027-01-04: "touch typing" was among one-off questions. Give the honest answer again, short.

I don't have exact prior wording; I'll answer substantively and note it's a repeat. Answer: yes, worth it, at any age; typing is motor skill, plateaus; 20 min/day for a few weeks; you're at maybe 30-40 wpm looking at keys, touch typing gets 60-80. Costs: a couple of weeks of being slower. Skip if you don't type much.

Honest: at your age — adults learn fine; it's one of the few skills with no age penalty, unlike languages. Only thing that matters is hours.

2027-01-11T01:18:07  thought   (empty)

2027-01-11T01:18:07  message   To the owner:
Yes, and your age has nothing to do with it. Touch typing is a motor skill, not a language or a puzzle — the part that slows older learners down in other things doesn't apply here. What it costs is hours, and there's no way round that.

Honest accounting: you're probably at 30-40 words a minute hunting and pecking, and you'd reach 60-70 in a couple of months at twenty minutes a day. The catch is the two weeks in the middle, where you're slower than you were and the whole thing feels stupid. Most people quit there, not at the start.

Worth it if you type more than an hour a day — spreadsheets count. Not worth it if you're at half an hour across a week; you'd never use the gains.

If you do start: cover your hands and don't look, use a proper typing tutor for the drills rather than trying to be careful in everyday work, and don't do it on the laptop keyboard if you can help it — the shallow travel makes bad habits easy to learn and hard to lose.

Asked this on Monday, fourth of the week — same answer, same numbers.

